In did_verify.rs:341, strip_did_sig_header returns out_lines.join('
').into_bytes(). If the input article used CRLF (
) line endings — the canonical NNTP wire format — the output bytes use bare LF (
). The author signed the original CRLF bytes. The verifier strips the DID-Sig header, gets LF-only bytes, and verifies against those. The signature covers different bytes than what was verified. This causes all valid NNTP-canonical (CRLF) articles to fail DID verification. Repro: sign an article with CRLF line endings, add DID-Sig header, pass to verify_did_sig — it will always return Ok(false) due to the byte mismatch.
